# L §4.3: "An at that gives a window (§4.4) reads the def once per law-date class the window overlaps ..., each at
# its first day inside the window. If all give one value, that is the value; otherwise it is HOLE-IMPRECISE, unless
# a windows clause chooses."  rate is 1 to 2026-03-15, 2 from 2026-03-16.  A window inside March 1-10 gives 1; a
# window across the change is imprecise; windows each splits it at the change, 1 on 03-10..03-15 and 2 on
# 03-16..03-20 (L §4.4; writer 8: windows latest is gone).
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"

input rate: int
input early: date
input across: date

def y1: int at early
  rule base = rate
def y2: int at across
  rule base = rate

version v1
  cite s
  governs 2026-01-01..2026-03-15
  known 2025-12-01
  rate = 1

version v2
  cite s
  governs 2026-03-16..
  known 2026-03-01
  rate = 2

version d
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  early = window(2026-03-01, 2026-03-10)
  across = window(2026-03-10, 2026-03-20)

ask q1: y1
ask q2: y2
ask q3: y2 windows each
=== expect
q1: 1
  cut 2026-12-31
q2: ?"date within window(2026-03-10, 2026-03-20) not precise enough for the law date of y2" (y2)
  cut 2026-12-31
q3 [across = 2026-03-10 .. 2026-03-15]: 1
  cut 2026-12-31
q3 [across = 2026-03-16 .. 2026-03-20]: 2
  cut 2026-12-31
